[17592186425116 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425116 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425116 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425117 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425119 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425119 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425119 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425119 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)-(x+y)^{3}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425119 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425119 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)-(1+0)^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =-1(y) \\\\ z+1 & =-y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0dfd5f35-4d13-5e8b-b12c-5286da4ef943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186268885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425122 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37de0ec1-dde9-55e5-b218-6452fee1d909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425123 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a385dd67-f4d9-5c4e-9dcb-07bacb547fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425124 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425124 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a445cd29-271b-5b63-aa35-8be905e25a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425124 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425125 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425123 #e